(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2608209600-cross-runtime-verification-and-the-libraries-it-required")
  (:adr/title "Cross-runtime verification, and the libraries it required")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-20")
  (:adr/body "## Context\n\nA session that began as \"what is the maturity of aiueos / amu / aya / kototama\"\nand became, by the owner's direction, two things: remove the JVM dependency,\nand build the basic libraries the workspace was reaching to the host for.\n\nMeasuring before building changed the answer three times, and the measurements\nare the part worth keeping.\n\n## What was measured\n\n**1. The JVM dependency is not in the source. It is in the verification.**\nAcross the five \u7dbe backends, all 70 test files are `.clj` and none is `.cljc`.\n`kotoba-kir` has 9 of 9 sources portable and ships a `cljs_i64.cljs`, and 31 of\n31 of its tests are JVM-only.\n\n**2. The foundational stdlib already existed.**\n`ADR-kotoba-lang-foundational-stdlib` is accepted and implemented -- 17 live\nrepositories with exactly the design rules the request asked for. `date` is\n`time`, bytes is `bytes`, digest is `hash`. Creating them again would have put\na second answer on the same plane. Only `i64` had no owner.\n\n**3. The exposure was workspace-wide, not per-library.**\nOf 22 stdlib libraries, 12 had tests and NO way to run them on a second\nruntime. Of the nine that could be made to run by hand, eight were green and\none was not.\n\n## What that hid\n\n**`kotoba-lang/bytes` shipped a SHA-1 that returned the wrong digest on\nClojureScript.** `sha1(\"abc\")` was `71347bf0...` instead of the FIPS vector\n`a9993e36...`. JavaScript's `>>>` masks the shift COUNT modulo 32, so a shift\nby 32 is a shift by zero, and the padding length block carried 24 in its high\nword instead of 0. The namespace header records that the ClojureScript side of\n`kotoba.turn.credential` calls it directly, so HMAC-SHA1 credentials computed\nthere did not match a compliant peer.\n\nThe comment directly above the broken line said \"a single 64-bit shift is not\ncljs-safe\" and split the length into two words for that reason. The split was\nright; the shift computing the high half was the same hazard again.\n\n**`kotoba-lang/lint` threw on every parse failure under ClojureScript** --\n`(.getMessage e)` inside a catch clause that was itself written portably as\n`#?(:clj Throwable :cljs :default)`. The reader conditional was applied to the\ncatch and not to the body. Separately, empty input returned the STRING \"nil\"\nas its canonical text, because cljs `edn/read-string` ignores the `:eof`\noption.\n\nBoth passed every JVM run they ever had, because a JVM run was the only run\neither had.\n\n## Decisions\n\n**Created exactly four libraries, each for a measured gap with no owner:**\n\n| repo | evidence | pinned |\n|---|---|---|\n| `i64` | 79 `Long/MIN|MAX_VALUE` + 187 `js/BigInt`, 4 repos | b0b383a |\n| `math` | 50 `Math/*`, no owner | 9aa8cc0 |\n| `strfmt` | 57 `(format ...)`, and `clojure.core/format` does not exist on cljs | cc35898 |\n| `reader` | `tools.reader` is JVM-only, so the workspace carried TWO readers for one grammar | 2803035 |\n\n**Did NOT create `nio`.** Its 633 references break down as 951 `Files/*` calls\n(769 in tests), 86 `StandardCharsets` and 11 `ByteBuffer`. `kotoba-lang/fs`\nalready owns that plane with a root-confined `#?(:clj java.nio :cljs node:fs)`\nhost. The one real gap the breakdown surfaced was `bytes` having `utf8-encode`\nand no `utf8-decode`, which is an addition rather than a repository.\n\n**Did NOT adopt `reader` yet.** Differencing it against `tools.reader` over all\n234 checked-in `.kotoba` files: 227 agree, and the remaining divergence is\ndeliberate float desugaring -- `1.0` becomes `(f64-from-bits ...)`. So it is\nnot a drop-in, and swapping the JVM path changes what the frontend receives.\nThat adoption needs its own evidence.\n\n## Verification, fixed\n\nEleven cross-runtime gates landed, ten of them green on the fleet with counts\nmatching the local measurements exactly:\n\n    bytes 18  json 27  scheduler 16  test 9  io 9\n    lint 8    time 8   lsp 8         http 7  fmt 6      = 116 tests on cljs\n\n`store` was ported (one `byte-array` in a test helper was the whole obstacle)\nand gated; its gate is REGISTERED and has not yet run.\n\nThree \u7dbe gates also landed and went green: kotoba-script 64/225,\nkotoba-wasm 95/425, kotoba-component 182/1250 -- and they are already gating\nopen PRs.\n\nEvery fix was verified to DISCRIMINATE rather than merely pass: reverting each\none was measured to turn the suite red, and restoring it green.\n\n## What is left, in priority order\n\n1. **`async.cljc`'s ClojureScript behaviour is unverified.** Its parity test\n   compares `.kotoba` against `.cljc` ON THE JVM, which is a different claim.\n   `async` and `hash` are JVM-only for real reasons (Chicory-hosted WASM, and\n   shelling out through `clojure.java.shell`), so this needs a new test rather\n   than a rename.\n2. **The four new libraries are adopted by nothing.** The 266 + 50 + 57\n   measured references disappear at adoption, not at creation.\n3. **`amu#625` is open and is the owner's call**: every one of the twelve\n   `kernel-compare-exchange-u32` call sites is the same binary try-lock, and\n   SMP is not up, so `kernel-cli`/`kernel-sti` would unblock 5 of 11 objects\n   today with no compiler change. The general primitive pins EAX in the x86\n   allocator and needs an LDXR/STXR retry loop on aarch64.\n4. **`bytes` and `lint` gates should be watched for their first red**, since\n   both defects were invisible to the runtime everything else runs on.\n\n## Note on method\n\nThree separate measurement harnesses in this session returned plausible wrong\nnumbers rather than failing: zsh not word-splitting a newline-joined variable,\nBSD `sed` emitting no trailing newline, and a `grep` prefix matching\n`io-ipld` when it meant `io`. Each was caught by reading the artifact instead\nof the intent. A first portability grep also answered 28 of 31 where a fuller\nsignal list answered 9 of 31 -- three times optimistic, because the absence of\na signal I had not thought to look for reads exactly like portability.")))
